From: pi-agent <noreply@sandbox>
Date: Sat, 05 Sep 2026 14:58:19 GMT
Subject: [PATCH] Draw ideal reference square analytically to remove corner divots

The "ideal (true square)" reference pane showed corner divots at
fractional square positions. Cause: the ideal was sampled on the lattice
and rendered through the same majority-vote pixel pipeline as the render
(pixel lit at >=3 of 6 samples). Edge pixels are partially covered by
construction — an open 1.0-length interval contains only 15 of 16 sample
columns — and a corner pixel combines a half-filled column with a
partially covered row, voting 2-of-6 and dropping out while its
neighbors pass. Systematic four-corner divots at any zoom level; not a
resolution problem.

Fix: draw the ideal pane analytically. Each display pixel is exactly
1/8 x 1/8 world units, so its ideal-coverage area is closed-form; pixels
shade from the checkerboard background to IDEAL_COLOR by that fraction,
which also reveals the sub-pixel edge phase. Applied to both the animate
common row and the pos-mode coverage zoom. The metrics are untouched —
they remain computed on the sample lattice; only the reference picture
changed, and it is now the exact square rather than a voted approximation
of it.

coverage.rs lerp/pane_from_colors exposed pub for the tool (the tool's
explicit imports shadow the glob-imported utility::lerp). Verified at
aligned and fractional positions; all workspace tests pass.

